  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The coastal flood risk will linger through mid-\nweek.\n\nCoastal flooding concerns continue across the region heading into \nthe new week in the wake of a departing nor'easter. While wind \ndirection may be less favorable than this weekend, still expect \nastro tides to remain elevated given the recent full moon. This will \nresult minor (to possibly moderate) coastal flooding with each high \ntide cycle (especially for the Charleston Harbor). \n\nCharleston Harbor: With the ongoing environmental conditions and \nrecently observed departures, tidal levels could reach ~7.3 ft MLLW \nduring this morning's high tide (925 AM). Thus, A Coastal Flood \nAdvisory will be in effect for Charleston and Coastal Colleton \nCounties from 7 AM until 12 PM. \n\nFort Pulaski: While tides will remain elevated at Ft Pulaski, they \nshould remain below minor flooding, peaking around 9 to 9.1 ft MLLW \nthrough mid-week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rising temperatures into mid week, remaining\nabove normal through the remainder of the week. \n\nUpper level ridging will build into the region through mid-week. \nToday will be slightly warmer than the weekend with highs in the\nmid-80s. Clear skies and light to calm winds will likely result\nin radiational cooling tonight with lows in the upper 50s \ninland and lower 60s across the coastal counties. As high \npressure builds over the region this week the forecast will \nlikely remain rain-free and feature a warming trend in \ntemperatures. The next chance of rain will be late week and into\nthe weekend.",
